From 638b9003cacfe1e2a1df0978c0d3c0e872f7be00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: show DomD's access points in Settings and follow the
 Wi-Fi toggle

Out of the box this guest is set up for virt_wifi, whose whole offer is
one synthetic SSID called "VirtWifi" -- and on this product even that
does not come up: cuttlefish's setup_wifi, which creates the interface,
is gated on ro.boot.wifi_impl=virt_wifi, which is empty here, so there
is no wlan0. Nothing real is reachable either way: the guest has no
radio, and the Wi-Fi HAL has nothing to scan with.

Replace it with a shadow AP. mac80211_hwsim gives the guest a simulated
radio; hostapd beacons, on that radio, the SSIDs DomD really scanned;
and the guest's own station associates with one of them, so the
Settings screen lists real access points and connecting to one puts
traffic on DomD's uplink through br-shadow. /vendor/bin/doma-shadow-ap
builds the hostapd config and brings the bridge up; init.xenvm-hwsim.rc
starts it once sys.boot_completed is set, and runs hostapd as a service
of its own, because init SIGKILLs a oneshot's process group when it
exits. With persist.vendor.doma.shadow_ap not true the script execs
setup_wifi instead and hands wlan0 back to virt_wifi, so falling back
costs a reboot rather than an SD rewrite.

wificond is installed as well. The inherited product set carries only
wificond_compat_symlink_module, which leaves /system/bin/wificond a
dangling symlink, and WifiNative needs wificond for the nl80211 setup
of wlan0, so without it enabling Wi-Fi fails at exactly that step.

The list is fed by /vendor/bin/doma-wifi-relay, which reads
newline-delimited JSON from DomD's agent over a virtio-serial port and
writes the scan results into the shadow AP's config. It also forwards a
connect request the other way, because a shadow AP cannot learn a
passphrase from a 4-way handshake -- only a MIC crosses the air -- so
on-screen entry and a real association cannot both hold unless the
passphrase is forwarded. This is lab use, not a product design, and the
app's source says so.

The relay also mirrors the station after a reboot: it reads the SSID
the station is on from `iw dev wlan0 link` and forwards that SSID's
connect once per boot. iw prints every byte outside printable ASCII, a
backslash and a space at either end as \xNN, so the relay decodes that
before it looks the SSID up in aps.conf, and it forwards nothing for an
SSID aps.conf has no row for. Checked on the PC, with iw's own
print_ssid_escaped() making the input, under bash, dash, busybox sh and
mksh R59, the last with toybox's printf as well: a Japanese SSID and
one with a space at each end are forwarded with their PSK, and one with
no row is not.

DomaWifiRelay is the DomA half: a privileged app that watches
WifiConfiguration's NetworkSelectionStatus for a wrong-password
disconnect and hands the typed passphrase to the relay through a
request file under /data/vendor/wifi/shadow/req. One failed handshake
disables a network permanently, so the app then calls
WifiManager.connect(), which clears that. It needs a privapp-permissions
allowlist entry: the platform key alone does not grant
READ_WIFI_CREDENTIAL, which is signature|privileged, and without the
entry system_server throws on every boot.

doma-shadow-aps.conf ships one placeholder row, and the file is
credentials storage rather than a cache: doma-shadow-ap exits if the
runtime aps.conf is empty, so something has to seed it. The shipped row
is EXAMPLE-SSID with an obvious placeholder passphrase; a real one is
entered on the board.

sepolicy buys attribution rather than access -- the board boots
permissive, so the scripts already run, but without an exec type every
avc line names init and audit2allow has nothing to work with.
BoardConfig.mk adds the directory; file_contexts gives both scripts one
exec type and labels /data/vendor/wifi/shadow hostapd_data_file, so the
apex hostapd can use it.

The channel has to be raw. Measured on the board: without
`stty raw -echo` on the channel fd the TTY echoes DomD's JSON back at
it, the agent reads its own messages ("ignoring malformed line"), its
writes time out, and the connect request never arrives -- so the AP
list is correct and the guest still cannot get out.

The relay also writes one newline right after the stty. Between opening
the node and the stty the TTY is still cooked with ECHO on, and the
node has had no reader until then, so DomD's queued publications are
partly echoed straight back; the fragment, which has no newline of its
own, corrupts the next line DomD reads. On this board that line was the
session's only connect request, which reached the agent as
b'{{"t":"connect",...}', was dropped, and left DomA with Wi-Fi but no
Internet (2026-09-23). The newline ends the fragment as a line of its
own that the agent drops, and the first real request arrives clean.

Both halves run while persist.vendor.doma.wifi_relay is unset or
"true", and they follow the Settings Wi-Fi toggle. An opt-in value
would live in /data, so every rewrite of the SD card would turn the
relay off again.

- doma-wifi-relay reads wlan0's flags every tick. With the toggle off
  the framework takes wlan0 down (IFF_UP clears, 0x1003 -> 0x1002
  within 1 s on the RPi4; carrier is then unreadable), and the relay
  holds PSK requests, the one path that asks DomD to associate while
  the station is down. aps publications keep syncing, so the list is
  current the moment the toggle is back on.
- It skips publications, and leaves requests from the previous boot,
  while doma_shadow_ap is still running, and writes aps.conf by rename,
  because init's SIGKILL at shutdown can land in the middle of the
  write.
- It idles in the virt_wifi fallback (persist.vendor.doma.shadow_ap not
  true), where there is no shadow AP: a publication would only
  crash-loop hostapd there.
- init starts it at sys.boot_completed, right after doma_shadow_ap.
- DomaWifiRelay is enabled on the same two values as init, returns
  early while Wi-Fi is off, and sweeps again on WIFI_STATE_CHANGED.

Measured on the PC with the relay's device interfaces stubbed, under
bash and under mksh (the shell the board runs it with): the toggle,
doma_shadow_ap running, fallback and previous-boot request scenarios
pass all 23 checks.

Both hostapd.conf generators, doma-shadow-ap at boot and the relay at
runtime, skip the same rows: an SSID that is still escaped (it has a
backslash) or longer than 32 bytes, and a PSK that is neither 8..63
bytes nor 64 hex digits, any of which would make hostapd refuse the
whole file. The skip is logged to stderr, not into hostapd.conf. A
64-digit hex PSK, which the AAOS dialog stores unquoted, is written as
wpa_psk=, and the relay refuses any other invalid PSK before it reaches
aps.conf or DomD. Checked on the PC under bash, busybox sh, dash and
mksh R59: both generators write the same file from one aps.conf, and it
carries no log line.

Wi-Fi starts off on a fresh /data and keeps the user's choice.
def_wifi_on is already false, but trout's trout-virtwifi-setup runs
`cmd wifi set-wifi-enabled enabled` at every boot, which also saves the
state, so the Settings toggle could neither start off nor stay off
across a reboot.

- An empty virtwifi.rc is installed over trout's. It is listed above
  the trout inherit, because for one destination the first
  PRODUCT_COPY_FILES entry wins and inherit-product puts the parent's
  entries at the inherit line. The note on trout's virtwifi in
  init.xenvm-buried-eth0.rc now mentions the replacement.
- doma-shadow-ap cycles Wi-Fi only while it is saved on (wifi_on 1 or
  2). The cycle is what makes the goldfish HAL, which lists wlan0/wlan1
  once when it starts, see the radios the script creates. With Wi-Fi
  off the framework never starts the HAL, and the user's first "on"
  lists them.

In fallback mode (persist.vendor.doma.shadow_ap not true) the empty
virtwifi.rc also drops the automatic connection to the open VirtWifi
network; the user picks it.

With Wi-Fi off DomA has no way out other than wlan0 as far as this tree
configures it (eth1 is a br-shadow port, and nothing routes
buried_eth0's 192.168.97.0/30), so a fresh image stays offline until
the user turns Wi-Fi on and joins an AP. adb over vsock is unaffected.

Measured on the PC with the step extracted and stubbed: it cycles for
wifi_on 1 and 2 and not for 0, 3, null or empty, under bash, busybox sh
and mksh.

